$schema: https://usm.dev/schema/v1.json
$id: smith-gray/audit-logging
$type: feature
$version: 1
$last_updated: "2026-09-22"
summary: |
  Every resolution is recorded — who, what scope, which jti, which host — without ever logging the
  secret. The audit log is append-only, captures denials as well as successes, and is the evidence
  trail that makes a security review and an incident response possible.
$system: smith-gray/system
$service: smith-gray/control-plane
status: built
intent: |
  A system that promises "the model never sees secrets" must be able to prove what happened without
  becoming a new place where secrets leak. An append-only record of identifiers and outcomes gives
  operators the accountability they need — including visibility of denials, which is often the first
  signal of an attack — while remaining worthless to anyone who steals it.
flows:
  - id: record-resolve
    name: Record a resolution attempt
    description: The sidecar reports each attempt to the audit log, successful or denied.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: observe
        target: Sidecar completes a resolve attempt with an outcome.
      - id: s2
        action: send
        target: >-
          Sidecar sends the record to the control plane — requester, scope, jti, object id, host,
          outcome, timestamp.
      - id: s3
        action: record
        target: Control plane appends the record; no plaintext is present in the payload.
      - id: s4
        action: observe
        target: The record is queryable but not modifiable.
  - id: query-audit
    name: Query the audit log
    description: An operator or auditor reviews resolution history.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: authenticate
        target: Caller authenticates with auditor or operator credentials.
      - id: s2
        action: send
        target: Query by object, jti, principal, host, outcome, or time range.
      - id: s3
        action: receive
        target: Matching records are returned; values are absent by construction.
contracts:
  - id: zero-plaintext-in-audit
    description: Audit records must never contain plaintext or key material.
    must_have:
      - Records carry identifiers and outcomes only
      - No record contains a secret value, DEK, or KEK
      - Stealing the entire audit log yields no secret
  - id: append-only
    description: Audit records cannot be altered or deleted through the application.
    must_have:
      - No update or delete endpoint exists for audit records
      - Records are immutable once written
      - Storage is retained according to policy
  - id: denials-recorded
    description: Failed attempts are recorded alongside successful ones.
    must_have:
      - Denied resolves are written with a denial outcome
      - Denial records name the requester and target
      - Denial-rate analysis is possible from the log alone
  - id: complete-attribution
    description: A record is sufficient to answer who used what, where, and when.
    must_have:
      - Requester identity, scope, jti, object id, host, outcome, and timestamp present
      - Records join to the issuance event for the token
      - No record is ambiguous about which object was involved
tests:
  - id: audit-contains-no-plaintext
    flow: record-resolve
    setup:
      secret_value: audit-test-secret
    expect:
      - assertion: no audit record contains the secret value
      - assertion: no record contains key material
    contracts:
      - zero-plaintext-in-audit
  - id: audit-is-immutable
    flow: query-audit
    setup:
      attempt_mutation: true
    expect:
      - assertion: no endpoint permits updating or deleting a record
      - assertion: attempts to mutate are rejected
    contracts:
      - append-only
  - id: denials-are-recorded
    flow: record-resolve
    setup:
      valid_token: false
    expect:
      - assertion: a record exists with a denial outcome
      - assertion: the record identifies the requester and target
    contracts:
      - denials-recorded
  - id: attribution-complete
    flow: record-resolve
    setup:
      valid_token: true
      injection_method: inject-env
    expect:
      - assertion: the record contains requester, scope, jti, object id, host, and timestamp
      - assertion: the record joins to the token issuance event
    contracts:
      - complete-attribution
decisions:
  - id: metadata-only-logging
    decision: Log identifiers and outcomes, never values or key material.
    rationale: >-
      The audit log is a high-value target precisely because it is central and long-lived. Keeping
      it metadata-only means a breach of the log is an accountability problem, not a
      secret-disclosure one.
    status: accepted
  - id: record-denials
    decision: Record denied attempts as first-class events.
    rationale: >-
      Denials are frequently the earliest evidence of misuse or a probing attack, and an audit log
      that only shows successes hides the signal an incident responder most needs.
    status: accepted
  - id: append-only-by-construction
    decision: Provide no mutation path for audit records rather than relying on access control alone.
    rationale: >-
      A capability that does not exist cannot be misconfigured. Immutability by construction is
      stronger than immutability by policy.
    status: accepted
implementation:
  primary: apps/control-plane/internal/store/store.go
  test_code: apps/control-plane/internal/inttest/control_plane_test.go
  test_code_status: manual
see_also:
  - smith-gray/sidecar-resolution
  - smith-gray/control-plane-api
  - smith-gray/token-revocation
